<body>
    <h1>AI+ EFK Stack</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>The Elasticsearch, FluentBit, and Kibana (EFK) stack is a powerf=
ul combination of open-source tools designed for centralized log management=
 and real-time visualization. It allows you to aggregate, process, store, a=
nd analyze logs from multiple sources, giving you deep visibility into your=
 systems.</p>
<p>Much like how <em>The Simpsons</em> set the standard for animated sitcom=
s, the EFK stack is the industry gold standard for log processing and analy=
tics. Here's why:</p>
<p>- Scalability: Elasticsearch can handle petabytes of data with distribut=
ed indexing and searching.<br>
- Real-time log processing: FluentBit processes logs in memory, ensuring lo=
w latency.<br>
- Powerful querying &amp; visualization: Kibana offers rich dashboards, ano=
maly detection, and interactive analysis.<br>
- Efficient resource usage: FluentBit is lightweight, making it ideal for r=
esource-constrained environments.<br>
 - Flexible integrations: Works seamlessly with Docker, Kubernetes, and clo=
ud platforms.</p>
<p>The purpose of this page is to provide a comprehensive walkthrough for s=
etting up the EFK stack for centralized logging. The goal is to collect log=
s from the AI+ containers, process them using FluentBit, and visualize them=
 in Kibana.</p>
<p>NOTE: Obviously, ALL system logs and analytics can be coalesced into thi=
s framework. As of this writing, we=E2=80=99re only focused on our utilitie=
s.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-Elasticsearch"><strong>Elasticsearch</strong></h1>
<p>The Elasticsearch components are not available in Ubuntu=E2=80=99s defau=
lt package repositories. They can, however, be installed with apt after add=
ing Elastic=E2=80=99s package source list.</p>
<p>All of the packages are signed with the Elasticsearch signing key in ord=
er to protect the system from package spoofing. Packages that have been aut=
henticated using the key will be considered trusted by the package manager.=
 In this step, we will import the Elasticsearch public GPG key and add the =
Elastic package source list in order to install Elasticsearch.</p>
<p>To begin, use cURL, the command line tool for transferring data with URL=
s, to import the Elasticsearch public GPG key into APT. Note that we are us=
ing the arguments -fsSL to silence all progress and possible errors (except=
 for a server failure) and to allow cURL to make a request on a new locatio=
n if redirected. Pipe the output of the <code>curl</code> command to the <c=
ode>gpg --dearmor</code> command, which converts the key into a format that=
 <code>apt</code> can use to verify downloaded packages.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -f=
sSL https://artifacts.elastic.co/GPG-KEY-elasticsearch |sudo gpg --dearmor =
-o /usr/share/keyrings/elastic.gpg
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>Next, add the Elastic source list to the <code>sources.list.d</code> dir=
ectory, where APT will search for new sources:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">echo "d=
eb [signed-by=3D/usr/share/keyrings/elastic.gpg] https://artifacts.elastic.=
co/packages/7.x/apt stable main" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/elas=
tic-7.x.list
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>The <code>[signed-by=3D/usr/share/keyrings/elastic.gpg]</code> portion o=
f the file instructs <code>apt</code> to use the key that we downloaded to =
verify repository and file information for Elasticsearch packages.</p>
<p>Next, update the package lists so apt will read the new Elastic source:<=
/p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t update
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Then install Elasticsearch with this command:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t install elasticsearch
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Elasticsearch is now installed and ready to be configured.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo na=
no /etc/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.yml
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The <code>elasticsearch.yml</code> file provides configuration options f=
or your cluster, node, paths, memory, network, discovery, and gateway. Most=
 of these options are preconfigured in the file but we can change them acco=
rding to our needs. For the purposes of our demonstration of a single-serve=
r configuration, we will only adjust the settings for the network host.</p>
<p>Elasticsearch listens for traffic from everywhere on port <code>9200</co=
de>. We will want to restrict outside access to our Elasticsearch instance =
to prevent outsiders from reading your data or shutting down your Elasticse=
arch cluster through its [REST API] (<a class=3D"external-link" data-card-a=
ppearance=3D"inline" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Representational=
_state_transfer" rel=3D"nofollow">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Representat=
ional_state_transfer</a> ). To restrict access and therefore increase secur=
ity, find the line that specifies <code>network.host</code>, uncomment it, =
and replace its value with <code>localhost</code> like this:</p>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">/etc/elasticsearch/elasticsear=
ch.yml</span></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">. . .
# ---------------------------------- Network ------------------------------=
-----
#
# Set the bind address to a specific IP (IPv4 or IPv6):
#
network.host: localhost
. . .
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We have specified <code>localhost</code> so that Elasticsearch listens o=
n all interfaces and bound IPs. If we want it to listen only on a specific =
interface, we can specify its IP in place of <code>localhost</code>. Save a=
nd close <code>elasticsearch.yml</code>.</p>
<p>These are the minimum settings you can start with in order to use Elasti=
csearch. Now we can start Elasticsearch for the first time.</p>
<p>Start the Elasticsearch service with <code>systemctl</code>. Give Elasti=
csearch a few moments to start up. Otherwise, we may get errors about not b=
eing able to connect.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl start elasticsearch
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>Next, run the following command to enable Elasticsearch to start up ever=
y time the server boots:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl enable elasticsearch
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>We can test whether your Elasticsearch service is running by sending an =
HTTP request:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 GET "localhost:9200"
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>We will see a response showing some basic information about your local n=
ode, similar to this:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">Output{
  "name" : "Elasticsearch",
  "cluster_name" : "elasticsearch",
  "cluster_uuid" : "n8Qu5CjWSmyIXBzRXK-j4A",
  "version" : {
    "number" : "7.17.2",
    "build_flavor" : "default",
    "build_type" : "deb",
    "build_hash" : "de7261de50d90919ae53b0eff9413fd7e5307301",
    "build_date" : "2022-03-28T15:12:21.446567561Z",
    "build_snapshot" : false,
    "lucene_version" : "8.11.1",
    "minimum_wire_compatibility_version" : "6.8.0",
    "minimum_index_compatibility_version" : "6.0.0-beta1"
  },
  "tagline" : "You Know, for Search"
}

</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<p>Elasticsearch allows for Index Lifecycle Management (ILM) to automate in=
dex retention. Initially, we=E2=80=99ll use 60 days as our default. To stor=
e logs 60 days and automatically delete old indexes, follow these steps:</p=
>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 PUT "localhost:9200/_ilm/policy/aiplus-policy" -H "Content-Type: applicati=
on/json" -d '{
  "policy": {
    "phases": {
      "hot": {
        "actions": {
          "rollover": {
            "max_age": "7d",
            "max_size": "50GB"
          }
        }
      },
      "delete": {
        "min_age": "60d",
        "actions": {
          "delete": {}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}'</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This policy:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Rolls over the index every 7 days or 50GB</strong>, whichever co=
mes first.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Deletes indexes older than 60 days</strong>.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Create an index template that uses the ILM policy:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 PUT "localhost:9200/_index_template/aiplus-template" -H "Content-Type: app=
lication/json" -d '{
  "index_patterns": ["aiplus-*"],
  "template": {
    "settings": {
      "index.lifecycle.name": "aiplus-policy"
    }
  }
}'</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This ensures that new indexes matching <code>aiplus-*</code> follow the =
retention policy.</p>
<p>Check that the ILM policy is correctly assigned to an index:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 GET "localhost:9200/aiplus-000001/_ilm/explain?pretty"</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>If correctly configured, we should see the ILM policy name in the output=
.</p>
<p>To delete an old index manually:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 DELETE "localhost:9200/aiplus-2024.01.01"</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To check disk usage for indexes, use the following command:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 GET "localhost:9200/_cat/indices?v&amp;h=3Dindex,store.size"</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-FluentBit"><br><strong>FluentBit</strong></h1>
<p>Fluent Bit is available as the <code>fluent-bit</code> package and suppo=
rts long-term releases of Ubuntu.</p>
<p>To install the most recent version, we can use the following script:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl ht=
tps://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit/master/install.sh | sh
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This script is a convenience helper and should be reviewed before use. F=
or a secure deployment, follow the steps below.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-SecureInstallationSteps"><strong>Secure Installation =
Steps</strong></h3>
<p>To ensure we receive the correct signed packages, we add the Fluent Bit =
server GPG key:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl ht=
tps://packages.fluentbit.io/fluentbit.key | gpg --dearmor &gt; /usr/share/k=
eyrings/fluentbit-keyring.gpg
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The updated GPG key (March 2022) has the fingerprint:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">C3C0 A2=
85 34B9 293E AF51  FABD 9F9D DC08 3888 C1CD
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We add the following entry to <code>/etc/apt/sources.list</code>, replac=
ing <code>${CODENAME}</code> with our Ubuntu release name (e.g., <code>foca=
l</code> for 20.04):</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">deb [si=
gned-by=3D/usr/share/keyrings/fluentbit-keyring.gpg] https://packages.fluen=
tbit.io/ubuntu/${CODENAME} ${CODENAME} main
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We refresh the package database:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t-get update
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To prevent issues with expired certificates, we upgrade the system:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t-get upgrade
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>If we encounter certificate verification errors, we should first ensure =
the <code>ca-certificates</code> package is installed:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t-get install ca-certificates
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We install Fluent Bit using:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t-get install fluent-bit
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
<p>Edit <code>/etc/fluent-bit/fluent-bit.conf</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">[SERVIC=
E]
    Flush        1
    Log_Level    info
    Parsers_File parsers.conf

[INPUT]
    Name              forward
    Listen            0.0.0.0
    Port              24224

# Apply AIPlus custom parser to JSON logs
[FILTER]
    Name              parser
    Match             docker.*
    Parser            aiplus-docker-json
    Key_Name          log
    Reserve_Data      true

[OUTPUT]
    Name              es
    Match             docker.*
    Host              localhost
    Port              9200
    Index             aiplus
    Logstash_Format   On
    Logstash_prefix   aiplus
    Retry_Limit       5
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">[PARSER=
]
    Name        aiplus-docker-json
    Format      json
    Time_Key    timestamp
    Time_Format %Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S

[PARSER]
    Name        aiplus-docker-plain
    Format      regex
    Regex       ^(?&lt;log&gt;.*)$
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><br>
Then, we start the service:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl start fluent-bit
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To confirm it is running, we check the service status:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">systemc=
tl status fluent-bit
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Expected output:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">=E2=97=
=8F fluent-bit.service - Fluent Bit
   Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/fluent-bit.service; disabled; vendor=
 preset: enabled)
   Active: active (running) since [timestamp]
 Main PID: [process_id] (fluent-bit)
    Tasks: 1
   Memory: [memory_usage]
      CPU: [cpu_time]
   CGroup: /system.slice/fluent-bit.service
           =E2=94=94=E2=94=80[process_id] /opt/fluent-bit/bin/fluent-bit -c=
 /etc/fluent-bit/fluent-bit.conf
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>By default, Fluent Bit collects CPU usage metrics and logs them to <code=
>/var/log/syslog</code>.</p>
<p></p>
<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-Kibana"><strong>Kibana</strong></h1>
<p>According to the <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://www.elastic.=
co/guide/en/elastic-stack/current/installing-elastic-stack.html" rel=3D"nof=
ollow">official documentation</a>, we should install Kibana only after inst=
alling Elasticsearch. Installing in this order ensures that the components =
each product depends on are correctly in place.</p>
<p>Because we=E2=80=99ve already added the Elastic package source in the pr=
evious step, we can just install the remaining components of the Elastic St=
ack using <code>apt</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ap=
t install kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>Then enable and start the Kibana service:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl enable kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p><span class=3D"legacy-color-text-inverse">Copy</span></p>
<p>Edit <code>/etc/kibana/kibana.yml</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">server.=
port: 5601
server.host: "0.0.0.0"
server.publicBaseUrl: "https://10.161.0.92:5601"
elasticsearch.hosts: ["http://localhost:9200"]
server.ssl.enabled: true
server.ssl.certificate: /etc/kibana/kibana-server.crt
server.ssl.key: /etc/kibana/kibana-server.key
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl enable --now kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Verify Kibana is running:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl status kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Open Kibana in a browser:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">https:/=
/10.161.0.92:5601
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To access Kibana directly via HTTPS (<code>https://&lt;remote-IP-address=
&gt;:5601</code>), follow these steps:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">openssl=
 genrsa -out kibana-server.key 2048
openssl req -new -key kibana-server.key -out kibana-server.csr
openssl x509 -req -days 1095 -in kibana-server.csr -signkey kibana-server.k=
ey -out kibana-server.crt
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro confluence-information-macro-inf=
ormation">
<span class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info confluence-informa=
tion-macro-icon"></span>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
<p>The STRONGLY PREFERRED alternative to self-signed certificates is a corp=
orate CA. We should request certificates from our customer=E2=80=99s intern=
al certificate authority if available.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Edit <code>/etc/kibana/kibana.yml</code>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">server.=
ssl.enabled: true
server.ssl.certificate: "/etc/kibana/kibana-server.crt"
server.ssl.key: "/etc/kibana/kibana-server.key"
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Ensure the proper file permissions!</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo mv=
 kibana-server.key kibana-server.crt /etc/kibana/
sudo chown kibana:kibana /etc/kibana/kibana-server.key
sudo chmod 400 /etc/kibana/kibana-server.key
sudo chmod 444 /etc/kibana/kibana-server.crt
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl restart kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo sy=
stemctl status kibana
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-"><strong> </strong></h4>
<h1 id=3D"AI+EFKStack-DebuggingandValidation"><strong>Debugging and Validat=
ion</strong></h1>
<p>Check FluentBit Logs</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo jo=
urnalctl -u fluent-bit -n 50 --no-pager
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Verify Elasticsearch Indexes</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 GET "http://localhost:9200/_cat/indices?v"
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Query Elasticsearch for Logs</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">curl -X=
 GET "http://localhost:9200/aiplus-*/_search?pretty=3Dtrue" -H 'Content-Typ=
e: application/json' -d '{"size": 10, "query": {"match_all": {}}}'
</pre>
</div>
</div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
